.TH MM41 1 "November 2026" "MM-41 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mm41 \- programmable RPN calculator for real and complex scalars, strings and matrices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mm41
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR EXPR ]...
.RB [ \-f
.IR FILE ]...
.br
.B mm41
.BR \-\-modules " | " \-\-commands " | " \-\-commands\-tex " | " \-\-version " | " \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mm41
is a modular stack-based reverse Polish notation calculator in the spirit
of the HP-41C and FORTH; it unifies the earlier MM-11, MM-14 and MM-15
calculators. The stack holds real and complex scalars, strings, and
real and complex matrices (GNU Scientific Library). It has 64 storage
registers, user-defined words with conditionals and loops, numerical
integration, root finding, minimisation and differentiation over those
words, polynomial roots, dates and sunrise/sunset, and HP-41 style
programs loaded from files.
.PP
With no arguments and a terminal on standard input it runs interactively
with GNU readline editing, history and tab completion; the stack is
printed after every line. Otherwise it runs in batch mode: the given
expressions and files, or standard input, are evaluated, the final stack is
printed, and the exit status is 1 if any error occurred.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " EXPR"
Evaluate
.I EXPR
as one line of input. May be repeated; expressions run in order.
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE"
Run the lines of
.IR FILE ;
.B \-
means standard input. May be repeated and mixed with
.BR \-e .
.TP
.B \-n
Do not load the saved words, the modules in the config directory, or the config at startup.
.TP
.B \-\-modules
List the built-in modules and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-commands
Print the complete command reference as Markdown (this is how COMMANDS.md
is generated) and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-commands\-tex
Print the command reference as LaTeX tables (used to build the manual) and
exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH INPUT
Input is whitespace-separated tokens. All input is case sensitive.
.TP
.B Numbers
.BR 3 ", " \-1.2e\-4 ", " .5
.TP
.B Complex numbers
.BR (1,2) ", " "(\-1.2e\-4, 0.7e2)"
(spaces inside the parentheses are allowed)
.TP
.B Strings
.BR \(dqhello\(dq ,
with the escapes \e\(dq \e\e \en \et.
.TP
.B Matrices
.B [rows cols $ elements...]
with real or complex elements, or
.B [rows, cols, \(dqfile\(dq]
to read one from a whitespace-separated text file.
.TP
.B Comments
.B \e
or
.B #
to the end of the line.
.TP
.B Execution tokens
.B @name
pushes a token for a command or word; the numerical methods and
.B exec
consume it.
.SH ERRORS AND UNDO
Each top-level token is atomic. If it fails, the stack is restored to its
state before that token, the error is printed on standard error, and the
rest of the line is skipped. A user word is one token for this purpose: a
word that fails half-way leaves no trace. In the REPL,
.B undo
restores the stack as it was before the previous line.
.PP
Real functions whose result would not be real promote their argument to
complex, consistently:
.B \-1 sqrt
is
.BR (0,1) ,
.B 2 asin
is
.BR (1.5708,1.3170) ,
.B \-8 3 inv ^
is
.BR (1,1.7321) ,
and a real matrix with one such entry becomes a complex matrix. Division by
zero is an error.
.SH COMMANDS
The full list, with stack pictures, descriptions and examples, is printed
by
.B listfcns
(grouped by category) and by
.BR "mm41 \-\-commands" ;
.B \(dqname\(dq usage
describes one command or shows a word's definition. The categories are
Arithmetic, Exp/Log, Trigonometry, Complex, Special functions, Constants,
Logic, Stack, Strings, Matrices, Polynomials, Numerics, Registers, Dates,
Display, Words, Programs and Session.
.SS Stack
.B dup drop swap over nip tuck pick roll depth clst print ps pm savestack loadstack.
.B n pick
copies the item at depth n (0 = top);
.B n roll
moves it to the top (1 roll = swap, 2 roll = rot).
.SS Registers
.B value n sto
stores
.I value
in register
.I n
(0\-63), as on the HP;
.B n rcl
recalls a copy. Registers hold any type.
.B pr
shows which are in use;
.B saveregs
and
.B loadregs
persist them.
.SS Words
.nf
: name body ;        \e" one line; redefinition replaces
\(dqname\(dq usage        \e" show the definition
listwords  n delword  clrwords  savewords  loadwords
.fi
A word is compiled when defined: unknown names are rejected at once, and
control flow becomes branches. A word may call itself; nesting stops at
256. Control words (inside definitions only):
.nf
flag if ... [else ...] then
begin ... flag until
begin ... flag while ... repeat
limit start do ... i ... loop        \e" skipped when start = limit
limit start do ... n +loop           \e" n < 0 counts down to limit inclusive
.fi
.B i
is the innermost loop index,
.B j
the next outer one. Flags are real numbers; 0 is false.
.PP
.nf
: fac  dup 1 gt if dup 1 \- fac * then ;
: sum  0 swap 1 + 1 do i + loop ;
.fi
.SS Modules
Every command belongs to a module;
.B modules
lists them with versions, and
.B \(dqname\(dq usage
names the module of a command. Built-in modules are compiled in
(\fBmake MODULES=...\fR chooses which). A
.I word module
is a file of definitions, one per line as \fIname body\fR or
\fB: \fIname body\fB ;\fR, with an optional first line
.BR "\e module NAME VERSION SUMMARY" ;
every
.I modules/*.mm
in the configuration directory is loaded at startup, and
.B \(dqname\(dq use
/
.B \(dqname\(dq unuse
load and unload one by name or path. The shipped
.B std
module (NPV, IRR, cumprod, sto_add, norms, locations, ...) is one;
.B listmacros
lists the words of the loaded word modules. A
.I compiled module
is a shared object exporting
.BR mm41_module() ;
.B \(dqfile.so\(dq load
loads one, as does placing it in
.IR modules/ ;
.B make shared-module DIR=...
builds one (see
.IR examples/modules/hello ).
.SS Numerical methods
A function is any word or command mapping x on the stack to f(x).
.nf
a b @f integrate     integral over [a,b] (tanh-sinh, bisecting where needed)
a b @f romberg       the same by Romberg extrapolation
a b @f fzero         root in a bracket with a sign change (Brent)
g   @f solve         root near one guess (bracket search, then Brent)
a b @f minimize      argument of the minimum in [a,b] (Brent)
x   @f deriv         f'(x) (Ridders' extrapolation)
lo hi @f fsum        sum of f(i), i = lo..hi;  fprod: product
@f exec              run f
.fi
.B x tol
sets the tolerance (default 1e-10, relative to max(1,|result|));
.B nevals
and
.B interr
report the evaluations used and the error estimate of the last integral.
The function must leave exactly one real; an error inside it aborts the
call. An integrable endpoint singularity converges to full precision when
that endpoint is 0.
.SS Programs
.B \(dqfile\(dq run
loads and runs an HP-41 style program: one instruction per line, with
.BR "LBL name" ", " "GOTO name" ", " "GOSUB name" ", " RTN ", " END
and tests ending in
.B ?
that skip the next line when false.
.B \(dqfile\(dq batch
runs a file of ordinary lines;
.B \(dqtext\(dq eval
evaluates a string.
.SS Shell
A line starting with
.B !
runs the rest as a shell command and pushes its output as a string.
.SH FILES
All in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mm41/ ,
which defaults to
.IR ~/.config/mm41/ :
.TP
.I config.txt
print precision, fixed/scientific, tolerance; written at exit.
.TP
.I modules/*.mm, modules/*.so
word modules and compiled modules, loaded at startup in name order.
.B make install
replaces the shipped word modules (such as
.IR std.mm ),
so keep your own words in
.I user_words.txt
or in a module of your own.
.TP
.I user_words.txt
words saved by
.BR savewords ,
loaded at startup.
.TP
.IR registers.txt ", " stack_backup.dat ", " .rpn_history
registers, saved stack and readline history.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; in batch mode 1 if any error was reported, 2 for a usage
error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ mm41 \-e \(dq2 3 + 4 *\(dq
[0] ℝ : 20.000000
$ mm41 \-n \-e \(dq[2 2 $ 4 7 2 6] minv pm\(dq
$ mm41 \-n \-e \(dq: f dup * 2 \- ;\(dq \-e \(dq0 3 @f fzero\(dq
[0] ℝ : 1.414214
$ echo \(dq(1,2) (3,4) *\(dq | mm41 \-n
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The user manual,
.IR mm41\-manual.pdf ,
with COMMANDS.md, MANUAL.md and CHANGES.md, is installed in
.IR PREFIX/share/doc/mm41 .
.BR readline (3),
the GNU Scientific Library manual.
